920		\[
921			C = \frac{\lceil a + b \rceil !}{(b \log 2)^{\lceil a + b \rceil}}
922			\qquad \text{and} \qquad
923			a' = a\bigl( (2c)^a + C \bigr)\;,
924		\]
925		both depending only on the universal constants $a$ and $b$; since $a \ge 1$, $c \ge 2$ and $C \ge 0$, one has $a' \ge a \ge 1$. The first term is at most $a(2c)^a (\log R)^a \eps^b \le a' (\log R)^{a'} \eps^b$, using $\log R \ge 2 \ge 1$ and $a \le a'$. The second term is at most $a(2c)^a (\log R)^{a - cb} \le a(2c)^a (\log R)^{-b}$, since $cb - a \ge b$ by the choice of $c$. For the third term, split $m^a = m^{a+b} \cdot m^{-b}$ and bound the first factor against the exponential: for all real $s$, all $t > 0$ and all $x \ge 1$, the Taylor estimate $y^n / n! \le e^y$ of the exponential, taken at $y = (t \log 2) x$ and $n = \lceil s \rceil$, gives
926		\[
927			x^s\, 2^{-tx} \le \frac{\lceil s \rceil !}{(t \log 2)^{\lceil s \rceil}}\;.
